  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Monday...\n\nUpper troughing over the western US and an associated low centered\nover northern Nevada was keeping diffluent flow over the region. \nA weak wave moving north into the area from the south will bring \nrain/snow showers (20-50% chance) and some drizzle/flurries \novernight into the morning hours, lingering over southeast MT into\nthe afternoon. While the chance remains low (less than 20%), \npockets of freezing drizzle cannot be ruled out around the Bighorn\nand Pryor Mountains into the morning hours, but warmer grounds \nshould limit this threat. This could (low chance) impact travel \nalong Highway 14 through the Bighorn Mountains. Any rain or snow\namounts will be light. With the recent moisture, patchy to areas \nof fog can be expected into the morning hours, with locally dense \nfog possible in spots. Otherwise, expect highs in the 50s today \nwith clouds clearing from the northwest to southeast. Fog is \npossible over portions of the area yet again tonight into \nWednesday morning. Lows tonight in the 20s to lower 30s are\nforecast.\n\nAs the aforementioned low tracks east into the central Rockies, an\nassociated wave lifting north and east through Wyoming looks to\nbring showers to the far southeast Wednesday afternoon and\nevening. Behind the wave, a dry cold front associated with a low\nnear Hudson Bay looks to move through the area on Thursday, \nbringing breezy northwest winds into the 20s mph for most areas, \nwith potential for gusts into the 30s mph over the east. High \npressure building over the region will bring generally warmer \nconditions for the weekend into early next week with highs\nincreasing into the 60s to lower 70s. Pressure gradients look to \ntighten along the western foothills Friday night into Saturday \nmorning, bringing the potential for increased winds from \nLivingston to Nye. A weak front crosses the area by Sunday\nbringing an end to the gap winds. Warm conditions continue for\nMonday. STP",
